n aura MinRange +3.␀␀␀DURATION: 3 hours.␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀In another time... I was␀␀␀a hero. In another␀␀␀time... I had Father's␀␀␀trust and Mother's love.␀␀␀I lost these blessings␀␀␀trying to end my␀␀␀troubles. Look around␀␀␀at what has come of us!␀␀␀Babylon is aflame, and␀␀␀many lives have been␀␀␀sacrificed in this war
␀␀␀gone awry.␀␀␀How could a Prince let␀␀␀this happen? Even as I␀␀␀led the Royal army into␀␀␀foreign lands I knew I␀␀␀would desert my␀␀␀countrymen. Such were␀␀␀the desperate moves␀␀␀of this desperate Prince.␀␀␀Since that time, armies␀␀␀attack Persia from north␀␀␀and south, east and␀␀␀west. And no matter␀␀␀where I run, the beast␀␀␀follows close behind.␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀It all began one year␀␀␀ago, when Persia waged␀␀␀war against its␀␀␀neighbors... It was the␀␀␀first time I had seen␀␀␀battle. The dead. The␀␀␀smoke. The clamor of a␀␀␀thousand desperate men␀␀␀passed by as I rode to␀␀␀meet Father at the front.␀␀␀I was unafraid in my␀␀␀ignorance. It was an␀␀␀unreal sight to such␀␀␀young eyes!␀␀␀India tried to stall us on␀␀␀the battlefields outside␀␀␀the capital, but Father␀␀␀and I led the troops␀␀␀further and further until␀␀␀finally reaching the␀␀␀Maharajah's palace.␀␀␀S   ␀␀␀+␀␀␀In battle, instinct is your greatest asset!&␀␀␀You have training - but no experience,␀␀␀my son.P   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀But that is what I seek! Isn't now the%␀␀␀perfect time to gain such experience?S   ␀␀␀␀␀␀There is no perfect time! Your#␀␀␀brashness bothers me. Although, the␀␀␀Indian army is feeble...P   ␀␀␀'␀␀␀I could take a small party north. Mount)␀␀␀Singri-la offers a clear vantage point of␀␀␀their capital!S   ␀␀␀#␀␀␀A bold beginning! If you were to do␀␀␀well in this battle -P   ␀␀␀"␀␀␀I know, I know - you promised me a'␀␀␀gift, but I do not want it. Please, let␀␀␀me prove my talents!S   ␀␀␀#␀␀␀I meant only to say that I would be%␀␀␀proud of you, son. That would be your␀␀␀gift to me.␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀There was a traitor in␀␀␀their midst - a flatterer␀␀␀who fooled Father too!␀␀␀It was the Vizier who␀␀␀showed us to his␀␀␀master's treasure␀␀␀vault. What did he want␀␀␀from us? He wanted the␀␀␀Hourglass! But when␀␀␀we would not give it to␀␀␀him, he tricked me into␀␀␀opening the Sands of␀␀␀Time!␀␀␀The Sands swirled␀␀␀throughout the palace,␀␀␀possessing everyone␀␀␀they touched with evil. I␀␀␀was forced to fight my␀␀␀own army or perish!␀␀␀In the end I was able to␀␀␀go back in time using a␀␀␀magical dagger, saving␀␀␀all I had slain. I thought␀␀␀my journey was over␀␀␀when I killed the␀␀␀treacherous Vizier␀␀␀- But I was wrong!␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀In the unspoiled days␀␀␀before that time I often␀␀␀walked with Darius␀␀␀through the splendid␀␀␀gardens of Babylon.␀␀␀What a beautiful setting␀␀␀for our debates! Each␀␀␀bloom was perfection;␀␀␀every leaf shimmered␀␀␀in delicious breezes.␀␀␀How could I not be␀␀␀happy?␀␀␀B   ␀␀␀ ␀␀␀What do you know of hardship? Of!␀␀␀war? Rose petals cushion the very␀␀␀soles of your feet!C   ␀␀␀'␀␀␀I consider this garden a noble's reward)␀␀␀for fighting well. You will see that I am␀␀␀a great warrior, like father.B   ␀␀␀#␀␀␀Prince, you know you haven't proven#␀␀␀yourself yet. Although we do need a␀␀␀second in command...C   ␀␀␀%␀␀␀I have more experience than you could&␀␀␀ever imagine, Darius. I say you should␀␀␀commission me for that post.B   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀O ho! You are quick! How far will your␀␀␀confidence lead you, Prince?C   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀I have confidence enough to wager my%␀␀␀honor. Do you think you can beat your␀␀␀pupil in battle?B   ␀␀␀␀␀␀Age versus beauty? I like it!␀␀␀B   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀Well fought, Prince! You have retained&␀␀␀your honor. Now, let us go back to the ␀␀␀gardens, I will tell you a tale!C   ␀␀␀!␀␀␀I prefer to focus on my training,␀␀␀friend! Shouldn't you?B   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀I am an old man... I could teach you!␀␀␀much if you would listen, Prince!C   ␀␀␀)␀␀␀I don't care for idle chatter! I would do'␀␀␀something grand if I could... I want to␀␀␀travel to faraway places!B   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀But... What could be more important?&␀␀␀Mark my words! You will miss our talks&␀␀␀once you're living the warrior's life!␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀That night I saw the␀␀␀beast for the first time.␀␀␀A black shadow fell␀␀␀across my path and␀␀␀attacked in an instant.␀␀␀Time stood still. My␀␀␀warrior instincts failed␀␀␀completely... But Darius?␀␀␀The great man was not␀␀␀intimidated. He threw me␀␀␀out of harm's way,␀␀␀shouting, "leave now!" as␀␀␀he fought furiously.␀␀␀My father was right... He␀␀␀told me many years␀␀␀ago that I did not have␀␀␀the instincts of a warrior.␀␀␀Darius did not have to␀␀␀die! Perhaps we could␀␀␀have overpowered the␀␀␀beast together. Instead I␀␀␀hesitated, terrified by the␀␀␀black shadow that hunted␀␀␀me.␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀What darkness had␀␀␀descended upon me?␀␀␀In the weeks that␀␀␀followed the beast's␀␀␀attack I found no rest␀␀␀from my worries... Or my␀␀␀regrets. Until finally, I␀␀␀found a slim volume in␀␀␀the great library called␀␀␀"The Box of a Thousand␀␀␀Restraints" which␀␀␀described a magical␀␀␀prison able to hold one␀␀␀hundred thousand souls.␀␀␀Surely this Box could hold␀␀␀the beast! To get it I had␀␀␀to take an army through␀␀␀India into Aresura. I knew␀␀␀this would anger the␀␀␀Maharajah, but it was the␀␀␀only way. I had to␀␀␀convince father to break␀␀␀our treaty with India,␀␀␀and I thought I knew how...␀␀␀S   ␀␀␀␀␀␀You think you can command an#␀␀␀army by virtue of your royal blood?␀␀␀Don't be foolish! You can't -C   ␀␀␀%␀␀␀But I can! And furthermore, I must! I#␀␀␀cannot stand by while the Maharajah␀␀␀insults our family!S   ␀␀␀ ␀␀␀The treaty was signed to promote#␀␀␀peace. And we have had peace since!C   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀I cannot be at peace when the province'␀␀␀of Khat is theirs. It is, after all, my'␀␀␀Mother's birthplace! It should be ours!S   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀Surprising! You never mentioned this$␀␀␀disquiet before. Darius was the only'␀␀␀one I trusted in matters of war, but...C   ␀␀␀ ␀␀␀There is no but! On who else can(␀␀␀Persia's fate rest now? I am the Prince,␀␀␀I am the future of Persia!S   ␀␀␀%␀␀␀Prove your words, my boy! We will see␀␀␀if you fight like a warrior!␀␀␀S   ␀␀␀(␀␀␀Prince - you are no warrior's son, but a#␀␀␀warrior in your own right. You have␀␀␀proven as much today.C   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀If I did well, then my mentor, General%␀␀␀Darius, deserves all of the credit...S   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀I had not planned for this to happen␀␀␀for a few years. Hm...C   ␀␀␀␀␀␀Yes? What are you thinking?S   ␀␀␀%␀␀␀Go see Arkabar in Mugger's Square. He'␀␀␀will be expecting you. He will give you␀␀␀a tattoo, the Mark of the King.C   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀The Mark of the King? A tattoo... like␀␀␀the one Darius wore?S   ␀␀␀'␀␀␀Yes. This will mark you as a general in"␀␀␀my Royal Army. Once the tattoo has␀␀␀healed - we ride to India!␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀That is how peace ended␀␀␀between India and␀␀␀Persia, a peace that had␀␀␀lasted a hundred years␀␀␀or more. As the Indian␀␀␀people ran to hide in mad␀␀␀confusion I realized that I␀␀␀was the only being alive␀␀␀who knew the true␀␀␀reason for our attack. I␀␀␀was absolutely alone,␀␀␀even in the middle of so␀␀␀many men. With the␀␀␀Mark of the King still␀␀␀burning on my sword␀␀␀arm, I fought my way␀␀␀through the panicked␀␀␀masses. I was going to␀␀␀save myself from the␀␀␀beast - at any cost.␀␀␀S   ␀␀␀(␀␀␀There is nothing sweeter than victory! I%␀␀␀will be proud to tell your mother how␀␀␀you tasted this reward today.F   ␀␀␀&␀␀␀I will stop to savor my successes when␀␀␀Aresura is ours, as well!S   ␀␀␀!␀␀␀Aresura? Victory has gone to your%␀␀␀head! We must take the Indian capital␀␀␀now or suffer a counterattack.F   ␀␀␀$␀␀␀I will press on and win more battles#␀␀␀in Aresura while you control India!S   ␀␀␀'␀␀␀We cannot fight this war on two fronts! ␀␀␀Do you know nothing of strategy?F   ␀␀␀%␀␀␀My path lies north, father. I mean to
␀␀␀follow it!S   ␀␀␀#␀␀␀Wait son! Do not desert us - or you␀␀␀will come to rue this day!␀␀␀G   ␀␀␀␀␀␀The road to Aresura␀␀␀was more arduous than␀␀␀any battle. Our blistered␀␀␀faces displayed our␀␀␀misery to the rocks, the␀␀␀sand and the infernal␀␀␀sun above. We had no␀␀␀other company... Save␀␀␀once, when a woman's␀␀␀tanned face and hooded␀␀␀eyes passed us by. Her␀␀␀strange manner was	␀␀␀puzzling.␀␀␀When her figure␀␀␀appeared before us I␀␀␀assumed I was seeing␀␀␀an apparition. Such␀␀␀things are said to have␀␀␀happened on long␀␀␀journeys through the␀␀␀Wounded Lands.␀␀␀I couldn't understand␀␀␀what she cried into the␀␀␀desert silence. I only␀␀␀recognized the words,
